checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:1:## 1. What You're Missing
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:5:- **Major, grade A: The legal hinge.** The page says the Court will decide whether the statute requires holding people “who are in the United States and were not admitted.” The dispute turns on two different phrases: **“applicant for admission”** and **“seeking admission.”** The [Eighth Circuit opinion](https://ecf.ca8.uscourts.gov/opndir/26/03/253248P.pdf) calls their relationship “the central inquiry”; the [Fourth Circuit opinion](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) explains that its petitioners contested the second condition. The correct account is that the government treats the phrases as equivalent, while the Second Circuit held that someone arrested in the interior long after entry is not then seeking admission.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:7:- **Major, grade A: The conflict is nationwide.** The page says only that “Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.” The [Fourth Circuit’s September 10 opinion](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) identifies nine other circuits on the opposite side and says “over five hundred” district judges had rejected the government’s reading in “over twenty thousand individual cases.” Those are the court’s descriptions, not independently audited counts of people detained. The correct account is a broad circuit split and a large volume of litigation, rather than a disagreement confined to the three circuits named on the page.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:9:- **Major, grade B: What the 2025 change displaced.** The page says, “In July 2025 the government changed its practice.” The [1997 Federal Register rule](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1997-03-06/pdf/FR-1997-03-06.pdf) expressly said that people present without admission “will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.” The [July 8 ICE guidance](https://iptp-production.s3.amazonaws.com/media/documents/2025.07.08_ICE_-_Interim_Guidance_Regarding_Detention_Authority_for_Applicants_0JGK0CH.pdf) instead says they are ineligible for a bond hearing before an immigration judge. The correct account is a reversal of a long-used custody practice through a new executive interpretation of the existing statute.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:11:- **Major, grade B: The grant leaves a constitutional question open.** The page says, “The question the Court agreed to hear asks whether the detention statute requires holding” this group. That is true, but the [government’s petition](https://www.justice.gov/d9/2026-07/barbosa_da_cunha_hold_pet_-_final.pdf) says a companion case, *Lopez-Campos*, “also squarely presents” whether that detention comports with due process. The [granted question](https://www.supremecourt.gov/qp/26-00104qp.pdf) names only 8 U.S.C. § 1225(b)(2)(A). The correct account is that review has been granted on the statutory question; the separate constitutional challenge remains unresolved by this grant.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:13:- **Major, grade B: The person and remedy at the center of the case.** The page says officers arrested Barbosa da Cunha “while he was driving to work” and that an immigration judge “released him on bond.” The pinned [Second Circuit opinion](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt:155) also says he had lived in the country for over twenty years, had work authorization and an asylum application under review, and that the government conceded he was neither a danger nor a flight risk. The correct account should explain that the disputed hearing is a chance to argue for release, **not automatic release**. His hearing resulted in bond; removal proceedings were still pending.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:23:## 4. Critical and Major Findings
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:25:| Finding | What the account says or leaves out | What the record shows and why it matters | Recommended addition |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:27:| **Major, A: statutory hinge** | “present in the United States and were not admitted” | The [Eighth Circuit](https://ecf.ca8.uscourts.gov/opndir/26/03/253248P.pdf) identifies whether an “applicant for admission” is also “seeking admission” as “the central inquiry.” That distinction determines which detention provision governs. | Explain both phrases and the competing readings in the main story view. |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:28:| **Major, A: national reach** | “Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.” | The [Fourth Circuit](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) lists nine circuits reaching the contrary statutory result and describes “over twenty thousand individual cases.” A reader otherwise cannot see why Supreme Court review matters nationally. | State the split and label the case figure as a court-reported litigation count, not a detention-population count. |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:29:| **Major, B: prior rule and policy change** | “In July 2025 the government changed its practice.” | The [1997 rule](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1997-03-06/pdf/FR-1997-03-06.pdf) expressly preserved bond eligibility for people present without admission. The [ICE guidance](https://iptp-production.s3.amazonaws.com/media/documents/2025.07.08_ICE_-_Interim_Guidance_Regarding_Detention_Authority_for_Applicants_0JGK0CH.pdf) removed bond hearings under the agency’s new reading. That contrast answers *what changed*. | Put the earlier rule beside the 2025 guidance. |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:30:| **Major, B: limit of review** | “The question the Court agreed to hear” is described only as detention under the statute. | The [petition](https://www.justice.gov/d9/2026-07/barbosa_da_cunha_hold_pet_-_final.pdf) identifies a companion case presenting due process as well; the [accepted question](https://www.supremecourt.gov/qp/26-00104qp.pdf) is statutory. The grant itself does not settle that other question. | Say explicitly what the Court accepted and what it has not agreed to decide here. |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:31:| **Major, B: hearing versus release** | “an immigration judge released him on bond” | The [Second Circuit opinion](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt:155) says the judge found no danger or flight risk. Section [1226(a)](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2024-title8/html/USCODE-2024-title8-chap12-subchapII-partIV-sec1226.htm) says the government **may** release an arrested person on bond. Winning access to a hearing does not itself require release. | Add one sentence explaining the hearing’s decision and the case’s individual stakes. |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:47:| The petition and response “were to be filed on paper only” | [Docket](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html) supports that filing instruction | Confirmed, time-limited | **Moderate:** the same docket gives new electronic-filing instructions after the grant. “Paper only” should not imply that the government petition cannot be read elsewhere; [DOJ publishes it](https://www.justice.gov/d9/2026-07/barbosa_da_cunha_hold_pet_-_final.pdf). |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:48:| CBS’s “tens of thousands” count is absent from the five records the page names | The page correctly limits its absence claim to those records | Confirmed within that corpus | **Moderate:** the [Fourth Circuit](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) reports over twenty thousand *cases*. That does not verify CBS’s count of immigrants detained without hearings. Neither figure should be substituted for the other. |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:52:- **Moderate, grade B: The rehearing pin is only the order.** The page says, “Judges Bianco and Nathan, joined by four others, concurred in that denial. Judge Menashi dissented.” The pinned [three-page order](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt:109) names additional opinions; the [full court document](https://ww3.ca2.uscourts.gov/decisions/OPN/25-3141_complete%20en%20banc_opn.pdf) includes Judge Schwartz’s statement, “Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc.” The correct account is that denial of full-court review was not unanimous agreement with the panel’s reasoning.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:54:- **Moderate, grade B: An available underlying filing is absent from the records list.** The page says the docket required the petition and response to be submitted “in paper form only.” The [DOJ copy of the petition](https://www.justice.gov/d9/2026-07/barbosa_da_cunha_hold_pet_-_final.pdf) includes the district court decision in its appendix and explains why the government preferred a different Supreme Court case. The correct account distinguishes a docket without an electronic petition link from a petition unavailable to readers.
