checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r3c-editor.md:83:9. **Minor, new. CUT:** “July 8, 2025 The appeals opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance on detention of applicants for admission.” A citation by a later opinion is not the dated change the timeline should carry.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r3c-editor.md:85:10. **Minor, residual. CUT:** “The Second Circuit counts Executive Branch practice over thirty years and across five administrations, and it says the Justice Department understood the detention statute the same way when it issued that rule.” The page has already explained the practice; this institutional aside delays the rehearing result.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r3c-editor.md:87:11. **Minor, new. CLARIFY:** “Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis.” His stated reason resolves the apparent contradiction in a few words.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r3c-editor.md:89:12. **Minor, residual. CUT:** “A motion to extend the time to respond was granted in Lopez-Campos, and Buenrostro-Mendez was rescheduled. Both were sent to the same September 28 conference.” The paragraph has already given the related petitions’ status as of October 3.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r3c-editor.md:107:Items **3 and 7 are Major**. The opening still asks a cold reader to parse exceptions before the case’s legal hinge, and the CBS heading temporarily turns a contested figure into the page’s own frame. The remaining numbered items are **Minor** copy decisions that make room for the explanation. The page is current against the checked dockets, but I would hold it for these edits.
