checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-05-r5-records.md:1:1. **Critical, new | Row 3, what-happened.** “The response and the reply are listed, and the docket does not include their text.” The [live Supreme Court docket](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html) now says “Brief of Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha submitted.” Its [linked document](https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/26/26-104/428500/20261005150833296_395847_tst_BRIEF.pdf) is titled “RESPONSE TO PETITION FOR A WRIT OF CERTIORARI” and says “Dated: August 21, 2026.” The sentence is false about the response after the October 5 upload. The September 4 reply remains listed without linked text.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-05-r5-records.md:3:2. **Major, new | Row 1, what-happened.** “It acted to correct that disparity and replaced the defined term entry with admission: the lawful entry of a person into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer.” “That disparity” points back to bond eligibility versus mandatory detention. The [House report](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CRPT-104hrpt469/html/CRPT-104hrpt469-pt1.htm) calls lawful admission the “pivotal factor in determining an alien's status” and says section 236(a) “restates the current provisions” for authority to “release on bond an alien who is not lawfully in the United States.” The cited [Second Circuit opinion](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt) says, “The key to understanding this is the focus on status rather than detention.” The page presents the government petition’s disputed account of Congress’s detention purpose as settled. The record supports the change from *entry* to *admission*; it does not establish that Congress thereby ended bond eligibility for people already inside the country.
