544-4. Constitutional avoidance The majority invokes the constitutional-doubt canon, which advises that “where a statute is susceptible of two constructions, by one of which grave and doubtful constitutional questions arise and by the other of which such questions are avoided, our duty is to adopt the latter.” U.S. ex rel. Att’y Gen. v. Del. & Hudson Co., 213 U.S. 366, 408 (1909). The canon has no place here because the correct interpretation of the detention provision at Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not “raise[] serious constitutional doubts.” Jennings, 583 U.S. at 286; see Demore v. Kim, 538 U.S. 510, 526 (2003) (describing the Supreme Court’s “longstanding view that the Government may constitutionally detain deportable aliens during the limited period necessary for their removal proceedings”). Indeed, the majority does not specify what it considers to be the serious constitutional problem posed by this interpretation. Instead it gestures at two inflated concerns that shrivel upon the slightest scrutiny. First, the majority asserts that the Government’s interpretation would subject aliens present in the country after illegal entry “to mandatory detention with no recourse to the constitutional protections that have long been held to apply to them.” Maj. Op. 49. That is incorrect. The Government’s interpretation would require that aliens not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted be detained without bond (but with the possibility of parole) during their removal proceedings. The majority undertakes no analysis of whether detaining these aliens with the option of parole instead of bond during the pendency of their removal proceedings would violate any due process rights they have concerning that temporary detention. Instead, the majority appears to assume that any
