549:detention of these aliens pending their removal would violate the Due Process Clause. That is wrong. See Demore, 538 U.S. at 531 (“Detention during removal proceedings is a constitutionally permissible part of that process” for aliens present in the United States.); see also id. at 523–531 (holding that due process does not require individual bond hearings for criminal aliens detained pending removal pursuant to Section 1226(c)); Reno v. Flores, 507 U.S. 292, 303–306 (1993) (holding that due process does not require individual “best interest” hearings for juvenile aliens detained pending removal); Carlson v. Landon, 342 U.S. 524, 537–542 (1952) (holding that due process does not require individual dangerousness or flight-risk findings to detain communist aliens pending removal). The Government’s interpretation of Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not curtail the constitutional limits on detention, which continue to apply. See, e.g., Zadvydas v. Davis, 533 U.S. 678, 690 (2001) (“A statute permitting indefinite detention of an alien would raise a serious constitutional problem.”). Second, the majority asserts that the Government’s interpretation does away with “well-established” “procedural safeguards” that apply before aliens “who have ‘passed through our gates, even illegally, may be expelled.’” Maj. Op. 47 (quoting Shaughnessy v. United States ex rel. Mezei, 345 U.S. 206, 212 (1953)). That is false. Neither party’s interpretation of the detention provision in Section 1225 has any effect on the procedural safeguards that DHS and the Executive Office for Immigration Review must follow before ordering a person removed from the country. See 8 U.S.C. § 1229a; 8 C.F.R. §§ 241.1– 241.15, 1240.1–1240.17.
