554-The majority clearly has strong views about what it considers the “moral implications” of denying individuals bond during their removal proceedings. Maj. Op. 47; see id. at 50–51 (comparing detention during removal proceedings to “internment camps”). But application of the constitutional-doubt canon must be grounded in the Constitution. C. Statutory History Statutory history also aligns with the conclusion dictated by the text and context: applicants for admission are seeking admission and Section 1225(b)(2)(A) reaches beyond aliens arriving at designated ports of entry. Petitioners’ speculation about congressional silence and reliance on agency practice cannot overcome the uniform direction of these authoritative guides to statutory meaning. 1. IIRIRA The changes that Congress made to the mandatory detention provision in IIRIRA refute Petitioners’ argument that it applies only to aliens at the border. Before 1996, the detention provision applied only to arriving aliens. Congress had used various formulations to communicate this limitation. As the majority recounts, the Immigration Act of 1893 provided: “[I]t shall be the duty of every inspector of arriving alien immigrants to detain for a special inquiry . . . every person who may not appear to him to be clearly and beyond doubt entitled to admission.” § 5, 27 Stat. 569, 570 (emphasis added). In the Immigration Act of 1903, Congress stated the detention obligation thusly: “Every alien who may not appear to the examining immigrant inspector at the port of arrival to be clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to land shall be detained for examination in relation
