160-                           B. Detention Under Section 236
161-
162-    Section 236 of the INA, 8 U.S.C.A. § 1226 (West 2025), provides
163-additional direction for the apprehension and detention of aliens “pending a
164-decision on whether the alien is to be removed from the United States.” INA
165:§ 236(a), 8 U.S.C.A. § 1226(a). Section 236 “generally governs the process
166-of arresting and detaining” aliens who are deportable under section 237(a),
167-8 U.S.C. § 1227(a) (2018), including “aliens who were inadmissible at the
168-time of entry or who have been convicted of certain criminal offenses since
169-admission.” Jennings, 583 U.S. at 288 (citing INA § 237(a)(1), (2), 8 U.S.C.
170-§ 1227(a)(1), (2)).
171-
172-    The detention provisions of section 236, distinguish between two groups
173-of aliens. The first group consists of aliens arrested on a warrant issued by
174-DHS, who, subject to certain restrictions, may be detained or released on
175:bond or conditional parole. INA § 236(a), 8 U.S.C.A. § 1226(a). The
176-regulatory provision at 8 C.F.R. § 1236.1(d) (2025) authorizes Immigration
177-Judges to “exercise the authority in section 236 of the [INA] . . . to detain the
178:alien in custody, release the alien, and determine the amount of bond, if any,
179-under which the respondent may be released, as provided in § 1003.19 of this
180-chapter.” The second group of aliens identified in section 236 are certain
181-defined categories of “criminal aliens” subject to mandatory detention under
182-section 236(c) of the INA, 8 U.S.C.A. § 1226(c). An Immigration Judge is
183:without authority to consider a bond request filed by an alien falling into this
184-category.
185-
186-    Section 236 does not purport to overrule the mandatory detention
187-requirements for arriving aliens and applicants for admission explicitly set
188-forth in section 235(b)(1) and (2) of the INA, 8 U.S.C. § 1225(b)(1), (2).
189-Thus, while an inadmissible alien who establishes that he or she has been
