206-present in the United States for over 2 years is not subject to the expedited
207-removal process, the alien nevertheless “shall be detained for a proceeding
208:under section 240.” INA § 235(b)(2)(A), 8 U.S.C. § 1225(b)(2)(A).
209-
210-              III. APPLICATION OF THE RESPONDENT
211-    The issue presented on appeal is one of statutory construction: Does the
212-INA require that all applicants for admission, even those like the respondent
213-who have entered without admission or inspection and have been residing in
214-the United States for years without lawful status, be subject to mandatory
215-detention for the duration of their immigration proceedings, and thus the
216:Immigration Judge lacks authority over a bond request filed by an alien in
217-this category? In addressing this issue, the Board must begin with the “plain
218-language” of the INA. See Jimenez v. Quarterman, 555 U.S. 113, 118 (2009)
219-(“As with any question of statutory interpretation, [the] analysis begins with
220-the plain language of the statute.”).
221-
222-    “The plainness or ambiguity of statutory language is determined by
223-reference to the language itself, the specific context in which that language
224-is used, and the broader context of the statute as a whole.” Robinson v. Shell
--
229-instead . . . read[s] the words ‘in their context and with a view to their place
230-in the overall statutory scheme.’” Matter of C-T-L-, 25 I&N Dec. 341, 345
231-(BIA 2010) (quoting FDA v. Brown & Williamson Tobacco Corp., 529 U.S.
232-120, 133 (2000)); see also Stenberg v. Carhart, 530 U.S. 914, 942 (2000)
233-(“When a statute includes an explicit definition, we must follow that
234-definition, even if it varies from that term’s ordinary meaning.”).
235-
236-    Under the plain reading of the INA, we affirm the Immigration Judge’s
237:determination that he did not have authority over the bond request because
238-aliens who are present in the United States without admission are applicants
239-for admission as defined under section 235(b)(2)(A) of the INA, 8 U.S.C.
240:§ 1225(b)(2)(A), and must be detained for the duration of their removal
241-proceedings. See Jennings, 583 U.S. at 300 (holding that the INA
242-“unequivocally mandates that aliens falling within the scope [of section
243-235(b)(1) and (2)] ‘shall’ be detained,” and that “[u]nlike the word ‘may,’
244-which implies discretion, the word ‘shall’ usually connotes a requirement”
245-(quoting Kingdomware Technologies, Inc. v. United States, 579 U.S. 162,
246-171 (2016))).
