The Supreme Court has understood the word “deemed” this way in a similarly phrased statute. In Sturgeon v. Frost, the Court considered a statute that provides: “‘Only those lands within the boundaries of any conservation system unit which are public lands (as such term is defined in this Act) shall be deemed to be included as a portion of such unit.’” 139 S. Ct. 1066, 1076 (2019) (quoting 16 U.S.C. § 3103(c)) (emphasis added). “As a matter of geography, both public and non-public lands” fell within unit boundaries. Id. at 1081. But because of the deeming language, “as a matter of law, only public lands would be viewed as doing so.” Id. As the Supreme Court explained, the term “deemed” “is used in legal materials ‘[t]o treat (something) as if . . . it were really something else,’” such as “by ‘deeming something to be what it is not’ or by ‘deeming something not to be what it is.’” Id. (quoting Black’s Law Dictionary 504 (10th ed. 2014)). In Sturgeon, the statutory phrase “[o]nly . . . public lands . . . shall be deemed to be included,” 16 U.S.C. § 3103(c), meant that “only public lands . . . would be considered part of” a conservation system unit, “even though” non-public lands also “in fact fall within [the unit’s] geographic boundaries,” Sturgeon, 139 S. Ct. at 1077 (emphasis added). As a result, non-public lands located inside system units were “declared outside them” for legal purposes, such that those lands were “no longer subject to the” National Park Service’s authority over system units. Id. at 1081. Thus, “deeming something to be what it is not” has real effect; it is not merely a matter of labeling. Id. (internal quotation marks omitted). The same is true here. An alien present in the United States who has not been admitted isn’t actually applying for lawful entry. And an arriving alien may not be either— he may be sneaking across the border or seeking lawful status, not admission. But as a

--- page 57 ---
57

matter of law for purposes of Chapter 12, all such individuals are to be regarded as applicants for admission. 3. Putting it all together Reading Section 1225(a)(1) and (b)(2)(A) together definitively answers the question presented. Section 1225(a)(1) tells us to “deem[]” Petitioners to be applicants for admission. Section 1225(b)(2)(A), which applies “in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission,” therefore applies to Petitioners. As already discussed, an applicant for admission is an alien seeking lawful entry into the country. See 8 U.S.C. § 1101(13)(A) (defining “admission”); American Heritage Dictionary 89 (“apply” means “[t]o request or seek”). As applicants for admission, Petitioners are “alien[s] seeking admission.” 8 U.S.C. § 1225(b)(2)(A); see Jimenez-Rodriguez v. Garland, 996 F.3d 190, 194 n.2 (4th Cir. 2021) (explaining that, because the petitioner was an “‘alien present in the United States who has not been admitted,’” “he qualifies as someone ‘seeking admission’” (first quoting 8 U.S.C. § 1225(a)(1), then quoting id. § 1182(d)(3)(A)(ii))). And they concede that an immigration officer determined that Petitioners are “not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.” 8 U.S.C. § 1225(b)(2)(A). Section 1225(b)(2)(A) therefore requires DHS to detain Petitioners for removal proceedings. 4. Petitioners’ error in reading Section 1225(a)(1) Petitioners and the majority disagree with this straightforward interpretation of the statutory language. Principally, Petitioners contend that “applicant” does not carry its ordinary meaning in Section 1225, and so, although Section 1225(a)(1) declares them to be “applicants for admission,” they nevertheless are not “seeking admission.” Their

--- page 58 ---
58

approach misreads Section 1225(a)(1) as a definition of the phrase “applicant for admission” and thereby strips that provision of its deeming function. First, Petitioners and the majority err by construing “applicant for admission” as a term of art that Section 1225(a)(1) defines. Section 1225(a)(1) does not articulate a new meaning for the phrase “applicant for admission,” introduced by the word “means” or “is.” See Groman v. Comm’r, 302 U.S. 82, 86 (1937) (concluding that the relevant section “is not a definition” because “when an exclusive definition is intended the word ‘means’ is employed”); Burgess v. United States, 553 U.S. 124, 130 (2008) (“As a rule, [a] definition which declares what a term ‘means’ . . . excludes any meaning that is not stated.” (internal quotation marks omitted)). Consider, by contrast, the INA section titled “Definitions,” which repeatedly defines statutory terms for purposes of the immigration laws by saying, “[T]he term [X] means [Y].” 8 U.S.C. § 1101(a)(1)–(h). Notably, Section 1225(a)(1) is not located in Section 1101(a), where Congress defined terms “[a]s used in” Chapter 12. Id. § 1101(a). Nor did Congress write Section 1225(a)(1) like it did those definitions. Doing so would have been simple enough; the provision would say, “For purposes of this chapter, ‘applicant for admission’ means an alien present in the United States who has not been admitted or who arrives in the United States (whether or not at a designated port of arrival and including an alien who is brought to the United States after having been interdicted in international or United States waters).” Of course, that is not what Congress wrote. Congress’s choice to describe two categories of aliens who “shall be deemed” applicants for admission does not define the term “applicant[s] for admission.” Consider

--- page 59 ---
59

an example. The sentence, “Tomatoes and cucumbers shall be deemed vegetables” does not define the term “vegetables.” Rather, it means that tomatoes and cucumbers shall be treated as though they are vegetables, despite being botanical fruits. The deeming sentence alters how we regard tomatoes and cucumbers; it does not alter the definition of vegetables or limit the meaning of “vegetables” to solely “tomatoes and cucumbers.” A vegetable remains a vegetable, and the deemed foods must be considered vegetables. The same would be true no matter what are deemed to be vegetables—be they botanical fruits, legumes, or chocolate chip cookies. The meaning of the term “vegetables” does not change in any of these scenarios, and its definition must be found elsewhere. Likewise here, “applicant for admission” isn’t defined to mean the two groups of aliens described in Section 1225(a)(1). The provision instead tells us how to regard those two groups of aliens for purposes of Chapter 12. Just as “vegetables” keeps its ordinary meaning, “applicant for admission” retains its definition as supplied by Section 1101(a)(13) and contemporary dictionaries. Section 1225(a)(1)’s deeming language conveys that, whatever “applicant for admission” means and whoever else it includes, the two identified groups of aliens shall be regarded as applicants for admission too. The majority posits that Section 1225(a)(1) is not a “deeming provision” because some of the aliens it identifies “are in fact . . . applying for admission[] into the country,” and there is “‘no need for Congress to provide’” that those aliens “‘be deemed’ applicants for admission” when they qualify as “applicants for admission” under “the ordinary meaning of the phrase.” Maj. Op. 24–25 (quoting Hedin v. Thompson, 355 F.3d 746, 749 (4th Cir. 2004)). In Hedin, on which the majority relies, this Court considered a provision

--- page 60 ---
60

that states, “‘Active service of commissioned officers of the [Public Health] Service shall be deemed to be active military service in the Armed Forces of the United States for purposes of all laws related to discrimination on the basis of race, color, sex, ethnicity, age, religion, and disability.’” 355 F.3d at 748 (quoting 42 U.S.C. § 213(f)). We rejected the argument that “active service” meant “active military service” because that interpretation would obviate the need for Congress to “deem” active service to be active military service. Id. at 748–749. The comparison to Hedin is inapt because Section 1225(a)(1) indisputably covers aliens who would not be “applicants for admission” within the ordinary meaning of that phrase. An “alien present in the United States who has not been admitted” is not actually applying for lawful entry. 8 U.S.C. § 1225(a)(1). Nor is an alien “who arrives in the United States . . . not at a designated port of arrival.” Id. The parenthetical phrase “whether or not at a designated port of arrival” sweeps in some aliens who, upon arriving at a designated port of arrival, actually do request lawful admission. Id. But that fact does not eliminate the need for the deeming provision or counsel against reading the statute according to its plain text.2 The majority also claims that “the word ‘deemed’ is used in different ways” throughout the INA and the United States Code more generally. Maj. Op. 26. Even if that

