were true, it does not absolve us of our responsibility to decipher how the word functions in the context of Section 1225(a)(1).3 Like all English speakers, Congress occasionally uses the same word in different ways, but we may not throw up our hands at the prospect of interpreting the written text. See, e.g., Pulsifer v. United States, 144 S. Ct. 718, 736 (2024) (observing Congress’s differing use of conjunctions even within the same statutory section). As explained above, the word “deem” means “regard as; consider”; “treat as if.” American Heritage Dictionary 487; Black’s Law Dictionary 415; 8 U.S.C. § 1225(a)(1) (title). And in Sturgeon, the Supreme Court demonstrated how to interpret and apply a similarly worded provision. Second, by construing Section 1225(a)(1) as a definition, Petitioners and the majority treat “applicant for admission” as a mere label, draining it of meaning and neutering Section 1225(a)(1)’s deeming function. Section 1225(a)(1) does more than dictate that every time we see the phrase “applicant for admission” we should understand it to include aliens arriving or present in the country without admission. By instructing that those aliens “shall be deemed” applicants for admission, Section 1225(a)(1) places those aliens in the posture of applicants for admission, with all that entails. As Sturgeon demonstrates, deeming one thing to be another as a matter of law has consequences. See

3 The majority cites TC Heartland LLC v. Kraft Food Groups Brands LLC, 581 U.S. 258 (2017), for the proposition that the word “deemed” sometimes “defines a term of art.” Maj. Op. 26. That’s not quite right. In TC Heartland, the Supreme Court considered statutes that defined the words “residence” and “reside” and then “deemed” a corporation to reside in the defined place for venue purposes. 581 U.S. at 262–263, 265–266, 269. The word “deemed” created the legal fiction that a corporation “resides” anywhere; it did not define the term “resides” or “residence.” See id. at 262, 266–267.

