139 S. Ct. at 1081. Otherwise, “[t]here is no reason” “to create a special legal fiction” in the first place. Id. at 1083. Put differently, Section 1225(a)(1) does not say that aliens arriving or present in the United States who have not been admitted “shall be called” applicants for admission. It says they “shall be deemed” applicants for admission. And an applicant for admission is an alien who “request[s]” or “seek[s]” lawful entry to the country. American Heritage Dictionary 89; see Webster’s New World College Dictionary 67; 8 U.S.C. § 1101(a)(13)(A). As an applicant for admission, therefore, an alien who illegally entered the country is necessarily considered to be “seeking admission.” 8 U.S.C. § 1225(b)(2)(A). Petitioners insist that they are not “alien[s] seeking admission” for purposes of Section 1225(b)(2)(A) because they are not actually seeking lawful entry to the country. That argument ignores the effect of Section 1225(a)(1). The statute deems aliens who actually are not applying for admission to be so. By deeming Petitioners to be persons who apply for admission, Section 1225(a)(1) deems them to be persons who seek admission. Any other conclusion disregards the ordinary meaning of the words “applicant” and “seeking.” See Webster’s New World College Dictionary 67, 1215; American Heritage Dictionary 89; Merriam Webster’s Collegiate Dictionary 56, 1057. There is no indication that Congress has assigned specialized definitions to those words in this context, therefore the ordinary meanings apply. Accordingly, although aliens present in the United States who have not been admitted are not actually applying for or seeking admission to the country, Section 1225(a)(1) tell us to regard them as though they are.

