immigration laws, “redundancies are common in statutory drafting—sometimes in a congressional effort to be doubly sure, sometimes because of congressional inadvertence or lack of foresight, or sometimes simply because of the shortcomings of human communication.” Barton v. Barr, 140 S. Ct. 1442, 1453 (2020); see also Pugin v. Garland, 143 S. Ct. 1833, 1842 (2023) (same); Al Otro Lado, 146 S. Ct. at 2092–2093 (“[E]xamples of redundancy [are not] uncommon.” (internal quotation marks omitted)). And “if forced to choose between an interpretation that entails some redundancy and one that contradicts what words usually mean, we [sh]ould choose the former.” Al Otro Lado, 146 S. Ct. at 2094. Indeed, Congress used this “belt and suspenders approach” another time in this very same statutory paragraph. Atl. Richfield Co. v. Christian, 590 U.S. 1, 14 n.5 (2020). Section 1225(a)(2) provides that “[i]n no case may a stowaway be considered an applicant for admission.” 8 U.S.C. § 1225(a)(2). Because Section 1225(b)(2)(A) applies only to “an alien who is an applicant for admission,” it could never apply to stowaways. But Congress expressly exempted them anyway. See id. § 1225(b)(2)(B)(iii) (“Subparagraph (A) shall not apply to an alien . . . who is a stowaway.”); id. § 1225(b)(2)(A) (“Subject to subparagraphs (B) and (C) . . .”). The repetition is redundant and unnecessary, but it does not nullify Congress’s intent to exclude stowaways from Section 1225(b)(2)(A), it clarifies it. With respect to the INA, then, “rigorous application of the canon [against surplusage] does not seem a particularly useful guide to a fair construction of the statute.” King v. Burwell, 576 U.S. 473, 491 (2015).

