admission.’”). In other words, applicants for admission are included in the broader group of aliens seeking admission. Congress used “or otherwise” the same way elsewhere in the same statute that enacted Section 1225(a)(3), the Illegal Immigrant Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act (IIRIRA). For example, one provision reaches anyone who “murders or otherwise causes death, bodily injury, or serious bodily injury to an individual.” Pub. L. No. 104208, Div. C, Title II, § 203(e)(2)(E)(i), 110 Stat. 3009-567 (1996). Murder is a way of causing death—it is included within the broader term that follows “or otherwise.” Another provision authorizes States to “prohibit or otherwise limit or restrict” alien eligibility for public assistance programs. 8 U.S.C. § 1624(a). Prohibiting is a way of limiting—again, the first term is included within the broader term that follows “or otherwise.” This consistent usage suggests a consistent meaning. See Sorenson v. Sec’y of Treasury, 475 U.S. 851, 860 (1986) (“The normal rule of statutory construction assumes that identical words used in different parts of the same act are intended to have the same meaning.” (internal quotation marks omitted)). And applying that meaning to Section 1225(a)(3), “applicants for admission” are “seeking admission.” Statements: Two paragraphs later, Section 1225(a)(5) provides, “An applicant for admission may be required to state under oath any information sought by an immigration officer regarding the purposes and intentions of the applicant in seeking admission to the United States, including the applicant’s intended length of stay and whether the applicant intends to remain permanently or become a United States citizen, and whether the applicant is inadmissible.” 8 U.S.C. § 1225(a)(5) (emphases added). This provision presumes that

