datory detention during such proceedings." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="except those “clearly and beyond a doubt entitled” to enter" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="“shall be detained” for further inquiry, unless they were granted parole." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="This case concerns several INA provisions addressing removal and detention that were enacted in 1996 as part of the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="In IIRIRA, Congress acted to correct that disparity." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Congress replaced the defined term “entry” with the term" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="“admission” is defined by statute as “the lawful entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization.”" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Instead of making physical entry into the United States the touchstone of an alien’s status, the “pivotal factor” became “whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.”" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="The government contends that Petitioner is subject to mandatory detention because he is an “applicant for admission” who “is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.”" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="the ordinary meanings of the phrases ‘applicant for admission’ and ‘seeking admission’ are the same." /></p>
