checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-06-r8-records.log:13244:**Minor, new | Row 4, what-happened.** “Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor.” The pinned [Justice Department petition](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt) says, “Members of Congress described the significance of that change as follows,” but the [House report](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CRPT-104hrpt469/html/CRPT-104hrpt469-pt1.htm) it quotes is absent from the manifest. The report’s exact wording is: “Hence, the pivotal factor in determining an alien's status will be whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.” The sentence’s attribution to lawmakers relies on the petition’s quotation rather than a pinned copy of their report. The report describes **status**; it does not by itself settle the disputed detention rule.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-06-r8-records.log:13252:**Minor, new | Row 4, what-happened.** “Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor.” The pinned [Justice Department petition](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt) says, “Members of Congress described the significance of that change as follows,” but the [House report](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CRPT-104hrpt469/html/CRPT-104hrpt469-pt1.htm) it quotes is absent from the manifest. The report’s exact wording is: “Hence, the pivotal factor in determining an alien's status will be whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.” The sentence’s attribution to lawmakers relies on the petition’s quotation rather than a pinned copy of their report. The report describes **status**; it does not by itself settle the disputed detention rule.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-06-since-44149b9a-referents.log:84:Before 1996, the procedures for detaining and expelling a person depended largely on whether the person had physically entered the United States. Someone who entered illegally without being caught was eligible for bond. Someone who presented for inspection at a port of entry was to be detained unless clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to enter, or unless granted parole. In 1996 Congress enacted the removal and detention provisions this case concerns. The Justice Department's petition says Congress acted to correct that difference, between people who had entered illegally and people who presented for inspection at a port of entry. Congress replaced the defined term entry with admission. Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor. The government contends that Barbosa da Cunha is subject to mandatory detention because he is an applicant for admission who is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted. It argues that "the ordinary meanings of the phrases 'applicant for admission' and 'seeking admission' are the same."
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-05-r7c-audit.log:505:</style></head><body><a class="skip-link" href="#page-content">Skip to content</a><div class="sheet"><header class="band"><div class="band-inner"><a class="wordmark" href="/">The Catch<b>.</b></a><span class="desc">A permanent record of checked claims</span><button class="nav-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="site-navigation">Menu <span aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><nav id="site-navigation" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/">Latest</a><a href="/events/" aria-current="page">Series</a><a href="/officials/">People</a><a href="/methodology/">How we check</a></nav><button class="search-toggle" aria-label="Search" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="global-search"><span>Search</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="10.5" cy="10.5" r="6.5"></circle><path d="m16 16 5 5"></path></svg></button><form class="global-search" id="global-search" action="/" role="search"><label class="sr-only" for="story-query">Search stories</label><input id="story-query" name="q" type="search" placeholder="Search stories" autocomplete="off"><button type="submit" aria-label="Search stories"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="10.5" cy="10.5" r="6.5"></circle><path d="m16 16 5 5"></path></svg></button></form><section id="search-results" class="search-results" aria-label="Matching stories" hidden><p role="status" aria-live="polite" data-search-message></p><ul data-search-matches></ul><a data-all-results href="/">View all results</a></section></div></header><div class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/immigration-detention/">Immigration detention</a><span>October 1, 2026</span></div><span id="page-content" tabindex="-1"></span><div class="content"><main class="story" id="story-root"><p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Immigration detention &middot; October 1, 2026 &middot; updated 2026-10-05</p><h1 data-layer="fact">The Supreme Court agreed to decide whether people already in the country who were never admitted must be held without a bond hearing</h1><p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">The Court&#39;s October 1 order takes the case and decides nothing yet. Since July 2025 the government has said people arrested inside the country who had entered without inspection must be held, and the appeals courts have split.</p><div class="mode-switcher" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Reading mode"><a class="mode-btn active" href="?mode=story" data-mode="story" role="radio" aria-checked="true">The story</a><a class="mode-btn" href="?mode=facts" data-mode="facts" role="radio" aria-checked="false">Just the facts</a><a class="mode-btn" href="?mode=work" data-mode="work" role="radio" aria-checked="false">Show the work</a></div><script type="module">document.addEventListener(`DOMContentLoaded`,()=>{let e=document.getElementById(`story-root`),t=document.querySelectorAll(`.mode-btn`);if(!e||!t.length)return;let n=new URLSearchParams(window.location.search),r=[`story`,`facts`,`work`].includes(n.get(`mode`))?n.get(`mode`):`story`;e.classList.add(`mode-${r}`);let i=e.querySelector(`[data-record-ledger]`);i&&(i.open=r===`work`),t.forEach(e=>{let t=e.getAttribute(`data-mode`)===r;e.classList.toggle(`active`,t),e.setAttribute(`aria-checked`,t?`true`:`false`),e.setAttribute(`tabindex`,t?`0`:`-1`)}),t.forEach((n,r)=>{n.addEventListener(`keydown`,e=>{let n=e.key===`ArrowRight`||e.key===`ArrowDown`?1:e.key===`ArrowLeft`||e.key===`ArrowUp`?-1:0;if(!n&&e.key!==`Home`&&e.key!==`End`&&e.key!==` `)return;e.preventDefault();let i=e.key===`Home`?t[0]:e.key===`End`?t[t.length-1]:t[(r+n+t.length)%t.length];i.click(),i.focus()}),n.addEventListener(`click`,r=>{r.preventDefault();let a=[...e.querySelectorAll(`section[id], aside[id]`)],o=a.find(e=>`#`+e.id===location.hash&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top>=0&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<innerHeight)??a.filter(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<=160).at(-1),s=n.getAttribute(`data-mode`);i&&(i.open=s===`work`),e.className=e.className.replace(/mode-\w+/g,``).trim()+` mode-${s}`,t.forEach(e=>{let t=e===n;e.classList.toggle(`active`,t),e.setAttribute(`aria-checked`,t?`true`:`false`),e.setAttribute(`tabindex`,t?`0`:`-1`)});let c=new URL(window.location.href);if(c.searchParams.set(`mode`,s),o){let e=a.indexOf(o),t=o.getBoundingClientRect().height?o:[...a.slice(0,e).reverse(),...a.slice(e+1)].find(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height);t&&(requestAnimationFrame(()=>t.scrollIntoView({block:`start`})),c.hash=t.id)}history.replaceState(null,``,c.toString()),document.dispatchEvent(new Event(`reading-mode-change`))})})});</script><nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact"><details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><ol><li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li><li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li><li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li><li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li><li><a href="#records">The records</a></li></ol></details></nav><section id="what-happened"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The grant</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. The detention statute deems a person present in the United States who has not been admitted an applicant for admission. The bond statute says the government "may release" a person on bond.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01/" data-passage="THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A.&#10;The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01/" data-passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/uscode-2024-1225/" data-passage="An alien present in the United States who has not been admitted" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence" data-passages="shall be deemed for purposes of this chapter an applicant for admission.">18</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-19" data-record-href="/records/uscode-2024-1226/" data-passage="may release the alien on" aria-label="Source 19: read the evidence">19</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">For about thirty years, by the Second Circuit's account, people who entered without inspection and were not admitted or paroled, and who were not apprehended while entering or shortly afterward or held on a criminal or terrorism ground, could ask for bond. On July 10, 2025, a memorandum from the commissioner of Customs and Border Protection said the detention statute covers them instead. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant them bond. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. An immigration judge denied him a bond hearing. A district court ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days. An immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit ruled the other way from the Board in Barbosa da Cunha's case.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="Executive Branch practice over thirty years" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="without inspection and admission&#10;who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.&#10;detain noncitizens on certain criminal or terrorism-related grounds">7</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06/" data-passage="aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence">7</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-11" data-record-href="/records/cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18/" data-passage="on July 10, 2025" aria-label="Source 11: read the evidence">11</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10/" data-passage="FROM: Rodney S. Scott" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence" data-passages="section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236, is the applicable immigration detention authority for all applicants for admission.">10</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-9" data-record-href="/records/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05/" data-passage="Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission." aria-label="Source 9: read the evidence">9</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="Petitioner-Appellee Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts&#10;Petitioner requested a bond hearing, which an immigration judge denied based on a determination that he was subject to mandatory detention&#10;The district court granted the petition and ordered the government to either provide a bond hearing or release Petitioner within ten days.&#10;presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond.&#10;we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.">7</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The detention statute uses two descriptions of the person, an applicant for admission and an alien seeking admission. It tells an immigration officer to detain that person for a removal proceeding when the officer decides the person seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/uscode-2024-1225/" data-passage="in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a></sup></p><figure class="quote-card" data-layer="fact"><figcaption class="qc-head"><span class="qc-speaker">Detention statute</span><span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span><span class="qc-when">2024 compilation</span><span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span><span class="qc-where">Inspection of applicants for admission</span></figcaption><blockquote class="qc-words"><p>in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/uscode-2024-1225/" data-passage="in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a></sup></p></blockquote><p class="qc-label">Government Publishing Office</p></figure><p data-layer="narrative">Before 1996, the procedures for detaining and expelling a person depended largely on whether the person had physically entered the United States. Someone who entered illegally without being caught was eligible for bond. Someone who presented for inspection at a port of entry was to be detained unless clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to enter, or unless granted parole. In 1996 Congress enacted the removal and detention provisions this case concerns. The government's petition says Congress acted to correct that disparity. Congress replaced the defined term entry with admission: the lawful entry of a person into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer. Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor. The government contends that Barbosa da Cunha is subject to mandatory detention because he is an applicant for admission who is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted. It argues that "the ordinary meanings of the phrases 'applicant for admission' and 'seeking admission' are the same."<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/doj-petition-26-104/" data-passage="Before 1996, the applicable procedures for detaining and expelling an alien under the INA depended largely on whether the alien had physically “entered” the United States" aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence" data-passages="Under that physical-entry-based regime, aliens who managed to evade detection and physically “enter” the United States illegally were subject to deportation proceedings and eligible for bond.&#10;Conversely, aliens who presented themselves for inspection at a port of entry on American soil were deemed not to have “entered” the United States.&#10;Those aliens therefore were subject to exclusion proceedings, and to mandatory detention during such proceedings.&#10;except those “clearly and beyond a doubt entitled” to enter&#10;“shall be detained” for further inquiry, unless they were granted parole.&#10;This case concerns several INA provisions addressing removal and detention that were enacted in 1996 as part of the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act&#10;In IIRIRA, Congress acted to correct that disparity.&#10;Congress replaced the defined term “entry” with the term">14</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="“admission” is defined by statute as “the lawful entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization.”" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence">7</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/doj-petition-26-104/" data-passage="entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer." aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence" data-passages="Instead of making physical entry into the United States the touchstone of an alien’s status, the “pivotal factor” became “whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.”">14</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="The government contends that Petitioner is subject to mandatory detention because he is an “applicant for admission” who “is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.”" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="the ordinary meanings of the phrases ‘applicant for admission’ and ‘seeking admission’ are the same.">7</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">Barbosa da Cunha's response, dated August 21, says that in the detention provisions Congress largely kept the earlier scheme. On that account, people arrested inside the country, including "those who had entered without admission," remained eligible for release on bond, and people apprehended at the border continued to be detained without the possibility of bond. He says Congress kept that difference even as the law changed the removal process, combining deportation and exclusion into one removal procedure and making the grounds depend on admission rather than on physical entry. He says the law also deemed certain people applicants for admission, including people present in the United States who had not been admitted, which put people who entered without inspection on the same footing in removal proceedings as other applicants for admission. Even with those changes, he says, Congress kept the core of the earlier detention scheme, including release on bond for people who had entered without inspection and detention without bond for people seeking admission at the border.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-6" data-record-href="/records/scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21/" data-passage="Dated: August 21, 2026" aria-label="Source 6: read the evidence" data-passages="In IIRIRA’s detention statutes, Congress largely preserved the preexisting detention scheme&#10;those who had entered without admission, remained eligible for release on bond&#10;Those apprehended at the border continued to be detained without possibility of bond&#10;even as IIRIRA made changes to the removal process.&#10;combining them into a single “removal” procedure&#10;not on physical entry into the United States, but instead on “admission,”&#10;“deemed” certain categories of noncitizens “applicant[s] for admission,”&#10;present in the United States [without having] been admitted&#10;This put noncitizens who entered without inspection on the same footing in removal proceedings as other “applicants for admission,”&#10;it retained the core of the pre-IIRIRA detention scheme.&#10;previously authorized “release on bond” for individuals who entered without inspection.&#10;Congress retained the prior rule of detaining, without possibility of bond, individuals who are “seeking admission” at the border">6</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization; he was already living here. So the court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Parole is a separate power. The parole statute lets the government release a person "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit". The July 10 memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="because he is not requesting lawful entry into the United States after inspection and authorization&#10;Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission&#10;who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.&#10;we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.">7</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10/" data-passage="may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole." aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence">7</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10/" data-passage="The only aliens eligible for a custody determination and release on recognizance, bond, or conditional parole under INA § 236(a) are aliens admitted to the United States" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p><figure class="quote-card" data-layer="fact"><figcaption class="qc-head"><span class="qc-speaker">U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit</span><span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span><span class="qc-when">April 28, 2026</span><span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span><span class="qc-where">Opinion</span></figcaption><blockquote class="qc-words"><p>Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence">7</a></sup></p></blockquote><p class="qc-label">Barbosa da Cunha</p></figure><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond under the detention statute. The Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond". The question the Court agreed to decide is whether the detention statute requires that detention. Whether the Constitution separately requires a hearing is not part of the question. The Fourth Circuit names eight courts, the First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh, that held the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the people who filed the cases, and that they must be given a bond hearing. On September 10, 2026, that court joined them. The Fifth and the Eighth held that the detention statute applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted, and that they must be detained without bond.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="applied for asylum in 2016 and was granted a valid work permit.&#10;has never been arrested for or charged with a crime.&#10;must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond">7</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01/" data-passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/doj-petition-26-104/" data-passage="In addition to the statutory question presented in this case, Lopez-Campos also squarely presents the question whether applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory-detention scheme to such aliens comports with due process." aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence">14</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-13" data-record-href="/records/ca4-bond-2026-09-10/" data-passage="Decided: September 10, 2026" aria-label="Source 13: read the evidence" data-passages="The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies to noncitizens inside our country&#10;like the Petitioners, and that they must thus be provided a bond hearing.&#10;Today we join the overwhelming majority of circuit courts of appeal that have considered this question&#10;The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand, that Section 1225 applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted to the United States&#10;they must be detained without bond">13</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The government's petition asked the Court to wait on this case. It said the Court should hold this petition pending another petition, Lopez-Campos, which it called a "better vehicle" for resolving whether holding those people is lawful, and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. The October 1 order granted this petition.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/doj-petition-26-104/" data-passage="The Court should hold the petition in this case pending the resolution of the petition in Lopez-Campos and then dispose of this petition as appropriate." aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence" data-passages="Lopez-Campos is a better vehicle than this case for resolving the lawfulness of applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens who are present in the country without having been admitted.">14</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01/" data-passage="The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="scotus-order-list-2026-10-01"><p>The Court's order list for Thursday, October 1, 2026, grants the petitions for review in a group that includes Philip L. Rhoney, acting director, against Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha. The petition names Rhoney as Acting Field Office Director of the Buffalo Field Office of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, substituted for his predecessor.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01/" data-passage="THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A.&#10;The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/doj-petition-26-104/" data-passage="Philip L. Rhoney, Acting Field Office Director of the Buffalo Field Office of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement." aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence" data-passages="Acting Field Office Director Rhoney is automatically substituted for his predecessor.">14</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01"><p>The question the Court agreed to hear asks whether the detention statute requires holding, during removal proceedings, people who are in the United States and were not admitted.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01/" data-passage="present in the United States without having been admitted" aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence" data-passages="CERT. GRANTED 10/1/2026">2</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01"><p>The Court's list of cases set for argument is dated October 1, 2026. This case is on it with a grant date of October 1. That line does not set a day for argument.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-3" data-record-href="/records/scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01/" data-passage="As of October 1, 2026" aria-label="Source 3: read the evidence" data-passages="RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA&#10;Granted: 10/1/26">3</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05"><p>The docket, opened July 23, 2026, records the petition that day, a response from Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha filed August 21, a reply from Philip L. Rhoney filed September 4, distribution for the September 28 conference, and a grant on October 1. On July 23 the same page said filings in this case should be submitted in paper form only, and should not be submitted through the Court's electronic filing system. The text of that response was posted on October 5. The docket lists that filing under August 21 and notes the PDF upload on October 5, and that entry includes a main document. That document is his response to the petition, dated August 21. The reply of September 4 is still listed, and the docket does not include its text. After the grant, the page says that if the Court schedules briefing and oral argument, the parties shall submit electronic versions of all prior and subsequent filings, and that subsequent filings should now be submitted through the electronic filing system.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05/" data-passage="Docketed: July 23, 2026" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence" data-passages="Aug 21 2026&#10;Brief of respondent Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha filed.&#10;Sep 04 2026&#10;Reply of petitioner Philip L. Rhoney filed.&#10;DISTRIBUTED for Conference of 9/28/2026.&#10;Petition GRANTED.&#10;should be submitted in paper form only, and should not be submitted through the Court's electronic filing system.&#10;If the Court schedules briefing and oral argument&#10;the parties shall submit electronic versions of all prior and subsequent filings&#10;Subsequent party and amicus filings in the case should now be submitted through the Court’s electronic filing system&#10;Brief of respondent Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha filed. (PDF upload - October 5, 2026)&#10;https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/26/26-104/428500/20261005150833296_395847_tst_BRIEF.pdf">5</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-6" data-record-href="/records/scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21/" data-passage="RESPONSE TO PETITION FOR A WRIT OF CERTIORARI" aria-label="Source 6: read the evidence" data-passages="Dated: August 21, 2026">6</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28"><p>Officers arrested him on September 26, 2025, while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. A judge then ordered a bond hearing or release, and an immigration judge released him on bond.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="On September 26, 2025, officers from U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="Norwood, Massachusetts&#10;provide him with a bond hearing or release him">7</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="the-catch"><aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact"><p class="tt-label">The catch</p><ol><li><h3>CBS's tens of thousands</h3><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during removal proceedings without bond hearings. The October 1 order, the Supreme Court's docket for this case, the Second Circuit's opinion, the Board of Immigration Appeals decision, and the July 10 Customs memorandum do not give a precise count of people held under the government's reading. The Fourth Circuit says district courts sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases, which counts court cases, not people in detention.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01/" data-passage="detain tens of thousands of immigrants during their removal proceedings without access to bond hearings" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-13" data-record-href="/records/ca4-bond-2026-09-10/" data-passage="sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases" aria-label="Source 13: read the evidence">13</a></sup></p></li><li><h3>Who the policy reaches</h3><p data-layer="narrative">Customs and Border Protection's public notice describes mandatory detention for applicants for admission the Department of Homeland Security encounters for the first time after they fail to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry. The July 10 memorandum says the first encounter counts "regardless of when or where that first encounter occurs". So the policy reaches a person arrested inside the country years after entering: Barbosa da Cunha was arrested driving to work in Norwood more than twenty years after he entered.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-11" data-record-href="/records/cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18/" data-passage="for the first time after failing to lawfully present for inspection at a U.S. port of entry" aria-label="Source 11: read the evidence">11</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10/" data-passage="regardless of when or where that first encounter occurs." aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country">7</a></sup></p></li></ol></aside></section><section id="where-this-sits"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The practice</p><h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2><figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>The 1997 Federal Register rule made people present without having been admitted or paroled eligible for bond.</strong></figcaption><figure class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th class>Date</th><th class>Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class>March 6, 1997</td><td class>A Federal Register rule says people present without admission will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.</td></tr><tr><td class>July 10, 2025</td><td class>Customs and Border Protection says its commissioner issued detention guidance to every component.</td></tr><tr><td class>Sept. 5, 2025</td><td class>The Board of Immigration Appeals decides judges lack authority to grant bond in this situation.</td></tr><tr><td class>Sept. 26, 2025</td><td class>Officers arrested the man in this case while he was driving to work.</td></tr><tr><td class>Apr. 28, 2026</td><td class>The Second Circuit affirms the order requiring a bond hearing or release.</td></tr><tr><td class>Sept. 10, 2026</td><td class>The Fourth Circuit names the appeals courts that require a bond hearing and the two that do not.</td></tr><tr><td class>Sept. 25, 2026</td><td class>The Second Circuit denies rehearing by the full court.</td></tr><tr><td class>Oct. 1, 2026</td><td class>The Supreme Court grants review in this case.</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></figure><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">On March 6, 1997, a Federal Register rule, effective April 1, said people present without having been admitted or paroled would be eligible for bond. On September 25, 2026, the full Second Circuit refused to rehear the case. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented, arguing that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis. He wrote that mere substantive disagreement with the panel opinion does not warrant the time and effort of full court rehearing.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06/" data-passage="Thursday, March 6, 1997" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence" data-passages="Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.&#10;Effective date: This interim rule is effective April 1, 1997.">12</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25/" data-passage="09/25/2026" aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence" data-passages="rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED.&#10;dissents by opinion from the denial of rehearing in banc.">8</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-15" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25/" data-passage="The government and Judge Menashi in his dissent from the denial of rehearing en banc argue that Section 1225 applies and that Petitioner must be detained without a bond hearing." aria-label="Source 15: read the evidence" data-passages="Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc.&#10;My “[m]ere substantive disagreement” with the panel opinion does not warrant the time and effort of full court rehearing.">15</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06"><p>Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06/" data-passage="Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination." aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28"><p>On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit affirmed the district court. It held that detention of a person in the United States after entering without inspection and admission, who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter, follows the bond statute, not mandatory detention. Judge Cabranes agreed and filed a separate opinion. The appeal was argued April 6.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="Decided: April 28, 2026" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="Argued: April 6, 2026&#10;we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.&#10;who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.&#10;Judge Cabranes concurs in the judgment and opinion of the Court and files a separate opinion.">7</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28"><p>In July 2025 the government changed its practice. The opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance dated July 8, 2025. The petition names that guidance as Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and says applicants for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025)" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence">7</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/doj-petition-26-104/" data-passage="Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025)." aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence" data-passages="may not be released from ICE custody” except by “parole”">14</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/" data-passage="Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494, 508 (5th Cir. 2026)" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence" data-passages="Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128, 1138 (8th Cir. 2026)">7</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25"><p>On September 25, 2026, the Second Circuit denied rehearing by the full court. Judges Bianco and Nathan, joined by four others, concurred in that denial. Judge Menashi dissented.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25/" data-passage="rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED." aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence" data-passages="dissents by opinion from the denial of rehearing in banc.">8</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05"><p>On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals held that immigration judges lack authority to hear bond requests, or to grant bond, for people present in the United States without admission.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-9" data-record-href="/records/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05/" data-passage="Decided September 5, 2025" aria-label="Source 9: read the evidence" data-passages="lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission.">9</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10"><p>The signed memorandum, a one-page scan, is addressed from Commissioner Rodney S. Scott. It says the department's position is that applicants for admission are subject to mandatory detention and may not be released except by parole.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10/" data-passage="FROM: Rodney S. Scott" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence" data-passages="SUBJECT: Detention of Applicants for Admission&#10;subject to mandatory detention under INA">10</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="unknowns"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">No argument date has been set. The Court took one question, what the detention statute means. Whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process is not part of it; the government's petition says a second case, Lopez-Campos from the Sixth Circuit, raises that question as well. The Court had not granted or denied review in that case or in a third, Buenrostro-Mendez from the Fifth Circuit, as of October 3.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-3" data-record-href="/records/scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01/" data-passage="RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA" aria-label="Source 3: read the evidence" data-passages="Granted: 10/1/26">3</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01/" data-passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/doj-petition-26-104/" data-passage="In addition to the statutory question presented in this case, Lopez-Campos also squarely presents the question whether applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory-detention scheme to such aliens comports with due process." aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence">14</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-16" data-record-href="/records/scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03/" data-passage="United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit" aria-label="Source 16: read the evidence">16</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03/" data-passage="United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence">17</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18"><p>Customs and Border Protection's public notice says Commissioner Rodney S. Scott issued guidance on July 10, 2025, and that applicants for admission encountering the department for the first time after failing to lawfully present for inspection at a port of entry are subject to mandatory detention.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-11" data-record-href="/records/cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18/" data-passage="on July 10, 2025" aria-label="Source 11: read the evidence" data-passages="subject to mandatory detention under INA Section 235">11</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="records"><details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>The question, the statutes, and one count in the coverage</summary><p>The question presented reads: whether the cited detention provision mandates detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like the respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01/" data-passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup></p><p>The 2024 compilation of the detention section says that if the examining officer determines an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained for a removal proceeding. The bond section says the government may release an arrested alien on bond of at least $1,500.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/uscode-2024-1225/" data-passage="not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-19" data-record-href="/records/uscode-2024-1226/" data-passage="bond of at least $1,500" aria-label="Source 19: read the evidence">19</a></sup></p><p>70 days from the July 23 petition to the October 1 grant. 450 days from the July 8 guidance date in the opinion to the grant. 448 days from the July 10 Customs guidance to the grant. 391 days from the September 5 Board decision to the grant. Counted from those dates.</p><p>CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants without bond hearings. That count is not in the October 1 order, the Supreme Court's docket for this case, the Second Circuit's opinion, the Board of Immigration Appeals decision, or the July 10 Customs memorandum.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01/" data-passage="detain tens of thousands of immigrants during their removal proceedings without access to bond hearings" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a></sup></p><p>The Associated Press wrote that the Court agreed on Thursday to review a policy denying bond hearings. The Hill wrote that the Supreme Court announced Thursday it will decide the legality of President Trump's mandatory detention policy.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/ap-detention-grant-2026-10-01/" data-passage="agreed to review a Trump administration policy denying bond hearings" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence">20</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-22" data-record-href="/records/hill-detention-grant-2026-10-01/" data-passage="announced Thursday it will decide the legality" aria-label="Source 22: read the evidence">22</a></sup></p></details><section id="records" data-layer="fact"><p class="sec-kicker">Sources</p><h2>The records</h2><p class="section-lede">Everything this page rests on, numbered where the story cites it. We keep a dated copy of each one.</p><h3 class="src-group">Primary documents</h3><ol class="src-list" start="1"><li id="src-1" value="1"><span class="src-n">1</span><a href="/records/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01/">Order list, October 1, 2026</a> · Supreme Court, 2026-10-01 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the grant of review</span></li><li id="src-2" value="2"><span class="src-n">2</span><a href="/records/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01/">Question presented</a> · Supreme Court, 2026-10-01 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the question the Court agreed to hear</span></li><li id="src-3" value="3"><span class="src-n">3</span><a href="/records/scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01/">Cases for argument, October 1, 2026</a> · Supreme Court, 2026-10-01 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the grant date and the absence of an argument day</span></li><li id="src-4" value="4"><span class="src-n">4</span><a href="/records/scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02/">Public docket, saved October 2, 2026</a> · Supreme Court, 2026-10-02 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the filing dates through the grant, as the docket stood on October 2</span></li><li id="src-5" value="5"><span class="src-n">5</span><a href="/records/scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05/">Public docket, saved October 5, 2026</a> · Supreme Court, 2026-10-05 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the docket after the response was posted, and the reply still listed without its text</span></li><li id="src-6" value="6"><span class="src-n">6</span><a href="/records/scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21/">Response to the petition, August 21, 2026</a> · Supreme Court, 2026-08-21 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; his account of the 1996 detention provisions</span></li><li id="src-7" value="7"><span class="src-n">7</span><a href="/records/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28/">Second Circuit opinion, April 28, 2026</a> · Second Circuit, 2026-04-28 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the decision under review</span></li><li id="src-8" value="8"><span class="src-n">8</span><a href="/records/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25/">Order denying rehearing, September 25, 2026</a> · Second Circuit, 2026-09-25 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the rehearing denial</span></li><li id="src-9" value="9"><span class="src-n">9</span><a href="/records/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05/">Board of Immigration Appeals, September 5, 2025</a> · Board of Immigration Appeals, 2025-09-05 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the bond-authority decision</span></li><li id="src-10" value="10"><span class="src-n">10</span><a href="/records/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10/">Customs memorandum</a> · Customs and Border Protection, 2025-07-10 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the signed detention memorandum</span></li><li id="src-11" value="11"><span class="src-n">11</span><a href="/records/cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18/">Customs notice of the July 10 guidance</a> · Customs and Border Protection, 2025-09-18 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the public notice of the guidance</span></li><li id="src-12" value="12"><span class="src-n">12</span><a href="/records/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06/">Federal Register rule, March 6, 1997</a> · Federal Register, 1997-03-06 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; written bond e