checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-05-r7c-audit.log:563:Before 1996, the procedures for detaining and expelling a person depended largely on whether the person had physically entered the United States. Someone who entered illegally without being caught was eligible for bond. Someone who presented for inspection at a port of entry was to be detained unless clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to enter, or unless granted parole. In 1996 Congress enacted the removal and detention provisions this case concerns. The government's petition says Congress acted to correct that disparity. Congress replaced the defined term entry with admission: the lawful entry of a person into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer. Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor. The government contends that Barbosa da Cunha is subject to mandatory detention because he is an applicant for admission who is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted. It argues that "the ordinary meanings of the phrases 'applicant for admission' and 'seeking admission' are the same."
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-05-since-59d9f20d-entailment.log:29:| 2 | what-happened | Before 1996, the procedures for detaining and expelling a person depended largely on whether the person had physically entered the United States. Someone who entered illegally without being caught was eligible for bond. Someone who presented for inspection at a port of entry was to be detained unless clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to enter, or unless granted parole. In 1996 Congress enacted the removal and detention provisions this case concerns. The government's petition says Congress acted to correct that disparity. Congress replaced the defined term entry with admission: the lawful entry of a person into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer. Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor. The government contends that Barbosa da Cunha is subject to mandatory detention because he is an applicant for admission who is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted. It argues that "the ordinary meanings of the phrases 'applicant for admission' and 'seeking admission' are the same." | doj-petition-26-104: Before 1996, the applicable procedures for detaining and expelling an alien under the INA depended largely on whether the alien had physically “entered” the United States; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Under that physical-entry-based regime, aliens who managed to evade detection and physically “enter” the United States illegally were subject to deportation proceedings and eligible for bond.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Conversely, aliens who presented themselves for inspection at a port of entry on American soil were deemed not to have “entered” the United States.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Those aliens therefore were subject to exclusion proceedings, and to mandatory detention during such proceedings.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: except those “clearly and beyond a doubt entitled” to enter; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: “shall be detained” for further inquiry, unless they were granted parole.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: This case concerns several INA provisions addressing removal and detention that were enacted in 1996 as part of the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: In IIRIRA, Congress acted to correct that disparity.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Congress replaced the defined term “entry” with the term; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: “admission” is defined by statute as “the lawful entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Instead of making physical entry into the United States the touchstone of an alien’s status, the “pivotal factor” became “whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: The government contends that Petitioner is subject to mandatory detention because he is an “applicant for admission” who “is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: the ordinary meanings of the phrases ‘applicant for admission’ and ‘seeking admission’ are the same.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt |
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-05-since-59d9f20d-entailment.log:8891:55:        <p data-layer="narrative">Before 1996, the procedures for detaining and expelling a person depended largely on whether the person had physically entered the United States. Someone who entered illegally without being caught was eligible for bond. Someone who presented for inspection at a port of entry was to be detained unless clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to enter, or unless granted parole. In 1996 Congress enacted the removal and detention provisions this case concerns. The government's petition says Congress acted to correct that disparity. Congress replaced the defined term entry with admission: the lawful entry of a person into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer. Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor. The government contends that Barbosa da Cunha is subject to mandatory detention because he is an applicant for admission who is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted. It argues that "the ordinary meanings of the phrases 'applicant for admission' and 'seeking admission' are the same."<Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Before 1996, the applicable procedures for detaining and expelling an alien under the INA depended largely on whether the alien had physically “entered” the United States" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Under that physical-entry-based regime, aliens who managed to evade detection and physically “enter” the United States illegally were subject to deportation proceedings and eligible for bond." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Conversely, aliens who presented themselves for inspection at a port of entry on American soil were deemed not to have “entered” the United States." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Those aliens therefore were subject to exclusion proceedings, and to mandatory detention during such proceedings." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="except those “clearly and beyond a doubt entitled” to enter" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="“shall be detained” for further inquiry, unless they were granted parole." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="This case concerns several INA provisions addressing removal and detention that were enacted in 1996 as part of the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="In IIRIRA, Congress acted to correct that disparity." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Congress replaced the defined term “entry” with the term" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="“admission” is defined by statute as “the lawful entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization.”" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Instead of making physical entry into the United States the touchstone of an alien’s status, the “pivotal factor” became “whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.”" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="The government contends that Petitioner is subject to mandatory detention because he is an “applicant for admission” who “is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.”" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="the ordinary meanings of the phrases ‘applicant for admission’ and ‘seeking admission’ are the same." /></p>
