## 1\. What You're Missing

As of October 5, 2026, 9:44 p.m. ET, I found **no Critical or Major omission**. The prior Major Genalo finding from immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-05-r7c-audit.md has been cured. The page now carries the dismissal of *Genalo v. G.M.*, the replacement *Genalo v. D.C.* petition, its October 21 response date, and counsel's characterization of the constitutional track. The live Rhoney, Lopez-Campos, Genalo v. D.C., and Buenrostro-Mendez dockets show no later disposition that the page is missing. [Supreme Court+2](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**Minor, repeat from immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-06-r8-records.md: the legislative-history sentence still reaches the House report through DOJ instead of the underlying record.**

Page bytes:

> “Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor.”

The pinned record, doj-petition-26-104.txt, quotes the House report as saying that the “pivotal factor” became “whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.” The underlying House report says exactly: “Hence, the pivotal factor in determining an alien's status will be whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.” [H.R. Rep. No. 104-469, pt. 1, at 225](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CRPT-104hrpt469/html/CRPT-104hrpt469-pt1.htm?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

The companion conference report says: “New section 236(a) restates the current provisions in section 242(a)(1) regarding the authority of the Attorney General to arrest, detain, and release on bond an alien who is not lawfully in the United States.” [H.R. Rep. No. 104-828, at 210](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CRPT-104hrpt828/html/CRPT-104hrpt828.htm?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**Correct account:** the House report made lawful admission pivotal to immigration **status**. That sentence alone does not settle which detention provision governs an interior arrest. The current story correctly presents the government's broader detention reading and Barbosa da Cunha's contrary reading side by side, so the remaining defect is source provenance and precision, not a missing side of the dispute.

## 2\. Is There More to the Story?

**No material additional story found.**

The backward search reached the pre-1996 detention regime, the 1996 enactment and competing legislative-history passages, the 1997 bond rule, and the long executive practice. The horizontal search covered the BIA decision, all eleven circuits identified in the Fourth Circuit's split, the related Supreme Court petitions, and the constitutional companion litigation. The forward search refreshed all four material Supreme Court dockets.

The story's core frame survives that reconstruction.

## 3\. Completeness Verdict

**Mostly complete with a minor omission.**

All A and B material in the reader model is carried in the story view. The earlier Major constitutional-history omission is patched. The remaining repeat Minor does not change any of the seven reader-model answers.

## 4\. Critical and Major Findings

**Critical: 0\. Major: 0.**

The prior Major in 2026-10-05-r7c-audit.md is no longer residual. The page now says that *Genalo v. G.M.* “was dismissed on September 11” and that “On September 21 the government filed a new petition, Genalo v. D.C., with a response due October 21.” Those facts match the pinned Supreme Court records and the September 14 and September 25 letters. [Supreme Court+1](https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/26/26-104/424172/20260914164958131_2026.09.14%20-%20Letter%20re%20Genalo%20Dismissal.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

## 5\. Before, After, and Around the Story

**Before:** Interior entrants who had entered without inspection were bond eligible under the old deportation regime. IIRIRA replaced entry with admission as a status concept in 1996\. The 1997 rule then expressly said people “present without having been admitted or paroled” remained eligible for bond. The Second Circuit describes that practice as lasting about thirty years.

**Change:** In July 2025 the government adopted the opposite detention reading, under which §1225 rather than §1226 governs applicants for admission even when the first encounter occurs years later in the interior. The BIA adopted that interpretation on September 5, 2025.

**Around:** By September 2026, the First, Second, Third, Fourth, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits were on the bond-hearing side; the Fifth and Eighth Circuits were on the government's side. The Fourth Circuit also described more than twenty thousand individual habeas cases, which is a case count and not a detainee census. [GovInfo](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCOURTS-ca4-25-07050/pdf/USCOURTS-ca4-25-07050-0.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**After:** Rhoney remains granted with no merits decision or argument date. Lopez-Campos, Genalo v. D.C., and Buenrostro-Mendez remain unresolved on the live dockets checked tonight. [Supreme Court+2](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

## 6\. Accuracy and Verification

I found **no material factual error** in the current page.

The August 21 Rhoney response is correctly described as filed August 21 with its PDF uploaded October 5\. The September 4 reply remains listed without linked text. The story correctly distinguishes a bond hearing from guaranteed release, correctly states Barbosa da Cunha's no-danger and no-flight-risk finding, and correctly limits the Supreme Court grant to the statutory question.

The sole Minor repeat is the House-report provenance issue described above.

## 7\. Source and Corroboration Problems

The October 1 grant has one controlling evidentiary lineage: the Supreme Court order and docket. AP, CBS, and The Hill are coverage of that act, not three independent proofs.

CBS's “tens of thousands” remains unverified as a count of people actually detained because of the July 2025 interpretation. The page handles that correctly by declining to substitute the Fourth Circuit's “over twenty thousand individual cases” for a detainee count.

The manifest contains **no video pin or transcript**, so there were no video frames to inspect.

The September 4 government reply remains unavailable through the live docket, and the obvious DOJ public brief paths did not expose a copy.

## 8\. Reconstructed Story

Before 1996, detention treatment largely turned on physical entry: people who had entered and were placed in deportation proceedings could seek bond, while people seeking entry at the border were generally detained. Congress replaced that entry framework with admission in 1996, but the legislative history does not unambiguously resolve this detention dispute. One report made lawful admission pivotal to status, while the conference report described the new §1226(a) as restating existing bond authority. The 1997 implementing rule expressly preserved bond eligibility for people present without admission.

That remained the executive practice for roughly thirty years. In July 2025 the government changed course, treating such people as subject to mandatory detention under §1225\. The BIA adopted that reading. Barbosa da Cunha, who had lived in the country for more than twenty years, was arrested driving to work, denied a bond hearing under the new interpretation, then obtained release after federal habeas relief and an immigration judge's finding that he posed neither danger nor flight risk.

The courts then split. Nine circuits ultimately applied the bond statute to interior residents in this position; the Fifth and Eighth Circuits applied mandatory detention. The Second Circuit's textual hinge was that Barbosa da Cunha could be an “applicant for admission” without presently “seeking admission.”

On October 1 the Supreme Court granted Rhoney to decide that statutory dispute. It has not decided the merits. The due-process question remains on a separate track involving Lopez-Campos and the Genalo litigation.

## 9\. Remaining Unknowns

The September 4 Rhoney reply is still listed without accessible text. No authoritative population series located in this audit isolates people detained specifically because of the July 2025 interpretation. Rhoney still has no argument date. Lopez-Campos, Genalo v. D.C., and Buenrostro-Mendez remain pending.

Those are genuine unknowns, but the current page identifies or properly bounds them. None leaves a high-probability material avenue unexplored that changes the reconstructed account.

VERDICT: COMPLETE
